	- extended numbered and named ACL

Extra Notes :

	* Advantage of Named ACL config mode
		- You can easily delete individual entries in the ACLs with <no> <entry-number>
		- You can't delete individual entries in Numbered ACLs, you can only delete the entire ACL
Editing Named ACL :

	* You can delete individual entries in the ACL with < no > < sequence-number >

	* You can insert new entries in between other entries by specify the sequence number

	* Resequencing ACLs

		- There is a resequencing function that help edit ACLs

		- The command is 
			R1(config)# ip access-list resequence 1 10 10 
	    change sequence number of the first entry to 10   <--|  |--> Add 10 for every entry after that

Extended ACLs :

	* Extended ACLs function mostly the same as standard ACLs

	* Can be Numbered or Named, just like standard ACLs

		-> Numbered ACLs ranges: 100 - 199, 2000 - 2699

	* Processed from Top to Bottom, just like standard ACLs

	* Can match traffic based on more parameters, so they are more precise (and more complex) than standard ACLs

	* Extended ACLs match traffic based on these parameters
		- Layer 4 protocol/port
		- source address
		- destination address

	* Protocols
		- <0-255>
		- ahp   ---> Authentication Header Protocol
		- eigrp ---> Cisco's EIGRP routing protocol
		- esp   ---> Encapsulation Security Payload
		- gre   ---> Cisco's GRE tunneling
		- icmp  ---> Internet Control Message Protocol
		- igmp  ---> Internet Gateway Message Protocol
		- ip    ---> Any Internet Protocol
		- ipinip --> IP in IP Tunneling
		- pcp   ---> Payload Compression Protocol

	* Matching Source / Destination IP address
		
		- To specify a /32 source or destination you have to use the host option or specify the wildcard mask
			# the address by itself wont work

	* Matching the TCP/UDP port numbers  

		- When matching TCP/UDP, you can optionally specify the source and/or destination port numbers to match

			TCP			  UDP
		   # FTP data(20)           # DHCP server(67)
		   # FTP control(21)	    # DHCP client(68)
		   # SSH(22)		    # TFTP(69)
		   # Telnet(23)		    # SNMP agent(161)
		   # SMTP(25)		    # SNMP manager(162)
		   # HTTP(80)		    # Syslog(514)
		   # POP3(110)
		   # HTTPS(443	
					    TCP & UDP 
					    # DNS

		 - Other options ( Not in the CCNA)
			# Ack ---> match TCP ACK flag
			# Fin ---> match TCP FIN flag
			# Syn ---> match TCP SYN flag
			# TTL ---> match packets with specific TTL value
			# Descp ---> match packets with a specific DSCP value

		- If you specify the protocol, source IP, Source Port, destination IP, destination port, etc a packet must match all of those 			  values to match the ACL entry
			* Even if it matches all except one of the parameters, the packets wont match that entry of the ACL

	  * Extended ACLs should be applied as close to the source as possible, to limit how far the packets travel in the network before being 	    denied
			- standard access List are less specific, so if they are applied close to the source there is a risk of blocking more 
			  traffic than intended
			

			







